dates.\n- **P: d4-d5**: Gains space and challenges the center, but may overextend without full development.\n- **Q: d1-d2** or **B: c1-g5**: Developing moves, but less impactful than immediate central strikes.\n- **N: b1-c3**: Natural development, but slower compared to active play on the kingside.\n\nReason: **N: f3-e5** maximizes activity and applies immediate pressure on a vulnerable point near Black’s king. It’s a principled move that follows opening theory—developing with tempo by attacking. Unlike pawn pushes or passive development, this knight jump creates concrete threats (e.g., Nxf7 after Bxf7+ Kxf7) and forces Black to respond carefully.\n\nAnswer: N: f3-e5"}
